import RequestProject.Epistemic.Repair
import RequestProject.Epistemic.ObserverJoin

/-!
# Governed residual learning: what a knowledge base may learn about itself

The upstream tranche states the boundary for the "the wiki solves itself over
time" workflow: a runtime may compute feature vectors, residual graphs and
clusters over a revision-pinned snapshot, and the question is what a consumer of
those outputs is allowed to do with them.  Upstream the boundary is a stack of
records whose boolean fields assert that nothing is edited automatically.

Here the workflow is built and the boundary is proved.

* **Cohort admission.**  `Wikidata.Admission` is the disposition of a candidate
  member and `Wikidata.Admission.trains` says whether it may refine the
  empirical invariant; `Wikidata.learned_ignores_untrained` proves that a held,
  ambiguous, unresolved, manually reconstructed or under-covered member changes
  nothing at all, rather than merely being recorded as excluded.
* **The learned invariant.**  `Wikidata.learned` is the executable invariant —
  the properties every admitted member carries — with the exactness theorem
  `Wikidata.mem_learned_iff`, monotonicity in the cohort
  (`Wikidata.learned_append_subset`), and `Wikidata.empty_cohort_learns_everything`:
  with no admitted members the invariant is vacuous, so coverage is a
  precondition and not a quality score.
* **Empirical is not normative.**  On a worked fragment a genuine instance
  violates the learned invariant (`Wikidata.learned_has_legitimate_exception`)
  and a non-member satisfies it (`Wikidata.learned_does_not_characterise`), and
  more sharply: class membership is not a function of the feature signature at
  all (`Wikidata.membership_not_factorsThrough_signature`), so no gate over
  resemblance can decide it.
* **Context gating.**  `Wikidata.edgeFor` sends an inadmissible or masked
  comparison to a masked analogy and an unknown one to an unknown-coverage edge,
  never to a similarity edge (`Wikidata.unknown_context_is_not_similarity`).
* **Merges.**  A merge candidate carries the normative and impact coordinates
  separately from the geometry; `Wikidata.mergeGate` passes only when all of
  them are discharged, and `Wikidata.gate_not_factorsThrough_geometry` proves the
  gate is not a function of the geometry, so convergence alone is never a merge.
  Enacting is review-gated (`Wikidata.enactMerge_eq_of_not_approved`,
  `Wikidata.enactMerge_eq_of_gate_failed`) and, unlike a redundancy pruning, a
  merge really does move the derived relations
  (`Wikidata.applied_merge_changes_instances`) — which is why it must be.
-/

